<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SECURITY NOTE (read before shipping this beyond your own device):

  cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" here disables Android's default
  block on plain-HTTP traffic, for every destination, app-wide. That is
  the deliberate, explicit trade-off for talking to a self-hosted vault
  server at a local-network IP with no TLS in front of it — the
  alternative was hardcoding a fixed IP/domain allowlist, which breaks
  the moment the phone changes networks or the server's LAN IP changes
  via DHCP.

  What this does NOT protect against: anyone else on the same LAN (open
  wifi, a compromised router, an ARP-spoofing neighbor) can read or
  tamper with the X-Vault-Token header and the file bytes in flight,
  even though the vault's *stored* content is still end-to-end
  encrypted at rest by the Go backend. The session token itself is
  effectively a bearer credential in cleartext on that network segment
  for the life of the session.

  Two ways to close this gap later, in increasing order of effort:
    1. Point the Go server's existing TLSCertFile/TLSKeyFile config
       (see config.json / main.go) at a self-signed cert, then pin that
       cert's public key here via <pin-set> instead of allowing
       cleartext at all.
    2. Put the server behind Tailscale/WireGuard and only ever reach it
       over that overlay network.
  Out of scope for milestone 1; flagging so it isn't forgotten.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
